# std/merge's image (design/caos-expr.md, Phase 3): this directory is a flake
# (flake.nix + flake.lock + worker), so its image is the flake-builder applied
# to the directory itself. It names the builder by a LOCAL deep-deps mount
# (DEEP-DEPS/flake-builder, declared in DEPS) -- the only way it CAN name it,
# since nothing resolves a builtin by name. Resolving this entry evaluates the
# expression, which makes the flake->image step explicit rather than leaving it
# to the server to detect.
#
# The `help` is bound HERE so that every harness offering this tool describes it
# the same way. It used to be a string literal inside llm-step, which meant a
# second harness had to copy it -- and a copied description drifts.
#
# `@param theirs` is the only thing a caller supplies. `ours` is the workspace
# it is merging into, which the launch binds; a model naming it would be naming
# the conversation it is already in.
HELP=<<END
Three-way merge another commit into the current workspace. `theirs` is a ref
name from the snapshot (e.g. `main`, `origin/main`) or a commit hash; the
current side is the workspace as it is now. A clean merge advances the
workspace to the merged result. A conflict advances it too, with git's inline
conflict markers in the files and a reserved `.caos/conflicts` file listing
every unresolved path -- including structural conflicts (delete/modify, mode,
binary) that have NO markers. Resolve each: edit the file (use `read` with the
stage's oid as `root` to inspect its content), then delete that path's rows
from `.caos/conflicts`. Then build and test.
@param theirs The commit to merge in: a ref name from the snapshot, or a commit hash.
END
IMAGE=run --base:@=DEEP-DEPS/flake-builder --in:@=. --lock:@=DEEP-DEPS/flake.lock
curry --base=$IMAGE --help=$HELP
